{"answer":"There are 7 such subsets:\n\n{1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}, {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, {2,3,4}.\n\nFor example, {1,8} sums to 9. A valid subset has two or three elements: one element would need to be 9, while four distinct positive elements have sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. The pair and triple casework below lists every possibility, so the count is exhaustive.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify each valid subset by cardinality, then enumerate the increasing pairs and triples whose sum is 9.","id":"cardinality-casework","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The size bounds prove that only the pair and triple cases remain; their direct enumeration is exhaustive."},{"description":"Partition valid subsets by largest element and enumerate the smaller elements needed to complete the sum.","id":"largest-partition","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This is also exhaustive because each nonempty finite subset has a unique largest element. It yields the same seven subsets as cardinality casework."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question asks: \"How many subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9?\"","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"given-instance","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The candidates are subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} whose element-sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step size-bounds.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["given-instance"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"size-classification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Every candidate subset has either two or three elements.","step_ids":["size-bounds"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step enumerate-pairs.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["given-instance","size-classification"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"pair-list","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The two-element candidates are exactly {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}.","step_ids":["enumerate-pairs"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in step enumerate-triples.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["given-instance","size-classification"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"triple-list","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The three-element candidates are exactly {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["enumerate-triples"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by combining the disjoint exhaustive size cases in steps size-bounds, enumerate-pairs, enumerate-triples, and total-count.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["size-classification","pair-list","triple-list"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"answer-count","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have element-sum 9.","step_ids":["size-bounds","enumerate-pairs","enumerate-triples","total-count"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"size-bounds","justification":"A one-element candidate would be {9}, but 9 is not in the ground set. A subset with at least four elements has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Thus only sizes two and three are possible.","statement":"Restrict candidates to subsets of size two or three."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["size-bounds"],"id":"enumerate-pairs","justification":"Write a pair as {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9. Then a<9/2, so a is 1, 2, 3, or 4; the corresponding b values are 8, 7, 6, and 5. These four pairs are admissible.","statement":"Enumerate all two-element candidates."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["size-bounds"],"id":"enumerate-triples","justification":"Write a triple as {a,b,c} with a<b<c. If a=1, then b+c=8 and b<c gives b=2 or 3, producing {1,2,6} and {1,3,5}. If a=2, then b+c=7 with 3<=b<c, forcing b=3 and c=4, producing {2,3,4}. If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12, impossible.","statement":"Enumerate all three-element candidates."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["enumerate-pairs","enumerate-triples"],"id":"total-count","justification":"The pair list has four subsets and the triple list has three. They are disjoint because their cardinalities differ, and step size-bounds excludes every other cardinality.","statement":"Add the exhaustive disjoint cases to obtain 4+3=7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
